      "name": null,
      "problem": "Calculate the number of terms in the following arithmetic series.\n\n$$\n5+15+25+…=180\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "To solve this problem, we use the formula for the sum of an arithmetic series:\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}=\\frac{N}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(N-1)d)\\,\\,\n$$\n\nWe have been given the sum ${S}_{N}=180$, and the first term ${a}_{1}=5.$ Our goal is to find $N,$ the number of terms.\n\nThe common difference $d$ can be found, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & ={a}_{2}-{a}_{1} \\\\  & =15-5 \\\\  & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these values into the formula for the sum of an arithmetic series, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{N} & =\\frac{N}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(N-1)d) \\\\ 180 & =\\frac{N}{2}(2\\cdot 5+(N-1)\\cdot 10) \\\\ 180 & =\\frac{N}{2}(10+10N-10) \\\\ 180 & =\\frac{N}{2}(10N) \\\\ 180 & =5{N}^{2} \\\\ 36 & ={N}^{2} \\\\ N & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we take the positive square root because $N$can't be negative. Therefore, our series has $N=6$ terms.",
